An idea for a new level. This will make no sense to you whatsoever if
you've never seen the film "Highlander".

Somewhere in the lower levels of the dungeon, one (and only one) new
artifact resides: The potion of quickening. If you drink it under
normal circumstances, you get the message "You feel no different".

However, if (deep breath):
- Your experience is above a certain level, 
- AND you own at least one named sword (Excalibur, Vorpal Blade,
Orcrist etc),
- AND you are at least 'skilled' in the use of that sword, 
- AND there are enough named swords left ungenerated (see later), 
- AND you are polymorphed to your original form 
- AND you have explored down to Dungeon level[big_number] and 
- THEN you drink the !oQuickening, you get the message "You feel
ancient powers rushing through you". It has no other effect, except to
create a magic portal right next to you. You can choose to ignore it,
or you could go through...

On entering the portal to the highlander level:
"Your <named sword> quivers expectantly." 
When you (w)ield the named sword: 
"There is an inscription on the blade- you never noticed it before.
Read inscription? (y/n)"
y
"The inscription reads: 'There Can Be Only One.'"

The level is laid out in a fairly linear pattern and teleporting/
digging are blocked, so you are forced to take a certain route. You
can turn back and leave the level at any time, but once you do the
portal closes. This is a one-time, all or nothing offer. The only
other inhabitants on this level are a number of master swordsmen of
various races, summoned from the far reaches of the globe for this
confrontation. Each one has a distinctive and appropriate arena to
fight in: Elven champion is surrounded by trees, dwarven warrior with
a mine-like rocky layout, vampire swordsman in a graveyard etc. Each
arena has at least one corpse, yielding an ordinary sword and a few
other trinkets.

Each swordsman is highly dangerous and wielding one of the named
swords so far not generated by the game. If the game has already
generated too many named swords (ie you have been wishing for them
left and right) then you can't access the level. If only one named
sword has been generated so far (ie the one you are carrying) then the
final swordsman is carrying a new, fairly powerful and otherwise
completely unobtainable (unwishable) named sword called "The Prize".

Now here's the good bit: On this level (and this level alone) you are
immune to any kind of death except beheading, and so are all the
master swordsmen. All named swords wielded by you or the swordsmen get
vorpal blade's decapitation attack for this level. You have to fight
your way through the swordsmen one by one, slugging away and
inflicting insane amounts of meaningless damage until one of you
scores the beheading. Since the chances of decapitating someone are
largely random (5% chance per successful hit, I believe), this would
be a deadly gamble for even experienced and high-powered players. All
your fancy armour, priest-protection, wands of death and cockatrice
corpses would be utterly useless. Your "oLS would still work, but you
could expect to use up quite a few and you really ought to be saving
them for the endgame. Some of the swordsmen would wear these as well.

So in the end, all you are fighting for is the kudos of having done
it, a cool (but not too cool) new weapon called The Prize, (which may
not even be there), ownership of a stack of named swords and the
considerable experience points that killing the swordsmen will give
you. Oh, there's the booty lying around on the corpses and any armour
the swordsmen might wear, but by the time you're qualified to fight
this level you should already have all that gear anyway. Even the
named swords wouldn't be that much of a bonus, since you've already
got one (unlikely to be only Orcrist or Werebane, since you have to be
at least skilled in its use) and your character and weapon choices are
likely to be pretty well established already.

Oh, I forgot to mention, the level should have some fire traps and an
underwater arena. You can't drown or be harmed by fire on the level,
but your inventory is still vulnerable. >=-)

All in all, the benefits of beating this level far outweigh the risks
involved for most rational people due to the arbitrary nature of
decapitation attacks. However for those curious or greedy enough to
give it a go, it might remind some of those uber-characters what it is
to sweat. Think of it as a voluntary late game challenge.

Possible issues, abuses and enhancements:
Pets: Pets should be allowed onto the level, but you should get an
opportunity to lock them in somewhere near the entrance. Otherwise
they will simply attack a swordsman and die. Unless your pet is
wielding Vorpal Blade..?

=oConflict: The swordsmen should stay seperate in their arenas so as
to make rings of conflict useless here. The corpses in the arenas
testify to the fact that they've all had to fight to get here, just
like you.

Holy ground: In the highlander films, the immortals can't fight on
holy ground. Maybe you could work a temple into the level somehow, but
I'm not sure how. This could cause a potential problem with
sacrificing the swordsmen corpses as well.

The E-word: Shouldn't work on the swordsmen, for obvious reasons. Same
for ?oScare Monster, other fear-type attacks.

Polymorphing yourself: Am i right inthinking that you could polymorph
into a nymph or something and go round and steal all the swords, thus
leaving the swordsmen defenceless? If so, polymorphing on this level
should remove your invulnerability to normal attacks.

Polymorphing the Swordsmen: This could also be a bit of a problem,
since you could just keep zapping until they reach a form that can't
wield a sword. On the other hand, there's always the chance that
you'll change them into something that doesn't have a head and then
run out of zap. Maybe the swordsmen should be poly-resistant (or
better yet, have poly control=-)

Classes: I believe some classes can't reach skilled in sword-like
weapons, which means they can't take part in the Highlander level.
Personally i think that's just too bad for them, but maybe some
special circumstances could be arranged. Failing that, isn't there
some way to advance a skill-level beyond your class' normal limit by
having a weapon as a divine gift or something like that?

Towels: You could wrap a blessed +5 towel around your neck to reduce
the chances of being beheaded;-)

